body:
I was able to reproduce the SQL syntax error. MySQL does not like the following order clause

``` sql
ORDER BY ((CONCAT(inside__comment, :c2)  ASC))
```

It will however accept

``` sql
ORDER BY CONCAT(inside__comment, :c2)  ASC
```

However, when using an expression object in `order()`, there doesn't seem to be a way to generate the specific bit of SQL. I was able to reproduce the SQL syntax error with:

``` php
        $table = TableRegistry::get('Comments');
        $query = $table->find();
        $inner = $table->find()->where(['id >' => 3]);
        $inner2 = $table->find()->where(['id <' => 3]);

        $concat = $query->func()->concat(['inside__comment' => 'literal', 'test']);
        $order = $query->newExpr($concat)->add('ASC')->type('');

        $query->select(['inside__comment' => 'Comments__comment'])
            ->from(['inside' => $inner->unionAll($inner2)])
            ->order($order);

        $results = $query->toArray();
        $this->assertCount(5, $results);
```

Given that objects cannot be array keys would accepting 'tuples' or additional arguments be a reasonable way to pair expressions and directions?

``` php
// As a tuple
$query->order([$expression, 'ASC']);

// Two arguments
$query->order($expression, 'ASC');
```
